00 light years  of each other in about 4.5 billion years  the two galaxies will then separate a  bit more and begin a gravitational dance  that brings them closer and closer  together  in the case of m33 Gaia has been able to  clock its rotation in Sky motion based  on 1500 of its most luminous stars  in 4.5 billion years m33 is projected to  move to an orbital position of 1.6  million light years far from the other  two spirals  let's now consider this future  interaction of Andromeda with the Milky  Way from interwoven perspectives  involving Hubble observations of distant  interacting galaxies  computer simulations of such galaxies  and artistic depictions of the future  night sky as viewed from Earth  We Begin by imagining what the night sky  will look like in about 4 billion years  at this time Andromeda will be nearing  its first close pass of the Milky Way  as a result it will be a huge feature on  the night sky rivaling the entire Milky  Way in brightness  at this point in time the spiral  structure of both galaxies will not yet  have been affected much by the  gravitational interaction  sadly it's unlikely that there will be  anyone on Earth to see this amazing sky  view in 4 billion years  by this time the sun is still a main  sequence star but its Luminosity will  have Arisen by 50 percent compared to  its current value  as a result all of the water on Earth  would have boiled away long before  thereby making the planet a hot lifeless  Rock  nevertheless the Universe goes on  as Andromeda and the Milky Way pass each  other over the following billion years  their gravitational traction begins to  pull tidal Tales of stars and gas out of  both galaxies  at this stage they will look something  like the pair of distorted interacting  spirals known as ARP 273  this beautiful rose-shaped pair of  galaxies is located in the constellation  Andromeda at a distance of 300 million  light years  the Hubble image of ARP 273 measures 230  000 light years across at this distance  the larger Galaxy is about five times  more massive than the smaller one  the distorted appearance of the spirals  in both galaxies indicates that they  recently suffered a close pass  the arms of the smaller Galaxy have been  tidally stretched into tails and a burst  of star formation has occurred in its  core  the outer arm of the larger Galaxy is  now a huge ring with blue bursts of star  formation scattered around it  the Hubble image of the interacting ARP  273 galaxies is thus a snapshot in time  of an initial close pass that has been  playing out for hundreds of millions of  years  at a similar stage the Milky Way and  Andromeda will provide a somewhat  comparable View From Afar  some differences may arise from their  smaller Mass ratio and more distant  first pass  as viewed from Earth after this first  pass the resulting night sky is likely  to feature a warped Milky Way studded  with blue bursts of star formation  the gas and stars and Andromeda will be  tidally stretched across the sky with  ongoing Starbursts throughout  at about the same time the Sun will have  begun its expansion to a red giant star  we are optimistically assuming in this  exercise that Earth survives this solar  transformation and is suitable as a  platform for nighttime viewing by  visiting astronomers  this video clip intermix is a computer  simulation of two colliding spiral  galaxies with Hubble images of actual  interacting galaxies at various stages  in their Collision  it begins shortly before the first pass  with a separation somewhat less than  what we expect for the initial Andromeda  Milky Way interaction  sharp Hubble images are absolutely vital  to refining such detailed simulations  in comparison the ground-based images of  distant interacting galaxies typically  look like fuzzy Blobs of light  a common feature generated by the first  close pass of two large spirals are  tidal Tales of gas and stars  as the clouds of gas in these Tails  collide with one another they can  produce bursts of star formation  by the time of a more direct hit in the  second pass the Galaxy cores are  typically surrounded by a bright  Starburst  the NGC 2623 pair of galaxies is in such  an interaction stage  its Hubble view is what the Milky Way  and Andromeda should look like from afar  in about 6 billion years  NGC 2623 is located in the constellation  cancer at a distance of about 250  million light years  the Hubble image is 240 000 light years  across at this distance with each tidal  tail stretching eighty thousand light  years  this image is the sum of multiple  exposures spanning 2.8 hours of  observing time  it's blue to red color scheme reflects  multi-wavelength observations taken from  the blue to the near infrared  let's zoom into the innermost 60 000  light years of this image  we see the nuclei of the two original  galaxies beginning to merge in the  center  they are surrounded by many bright blue  star clusters that extend into the tidal  Tales  it turns out that the overall star  formation rate in this galaxy merger is  about 20 times that of the Milky Way  induced by many Cloud collisions this  Starburst is rapidly converting the  remaining gas in these galaxies into  Stars  a classic example of a collision  Starburst is a Galaxy pair known as the  antennae galaxies  they are located in the southern  constellation corvus at a distance of 70  million light years  Hubble has imaged the innermost sixty  thousand light years of the antennae at  its highest resolution  it reveals the old yellow cores of two  spiral galaxies amidst the tangle of  bright blue star clusters pink hydrogen  nebulae and brown dust clouds  among the Thousand massive Young Star  clusters identified in this image  about a hundred are compact enough to  eventually become globular clusters  like the previous example the overall  antennae star formation rate is also  about 20 times that of the Milky Way  based on these two cases the entire  night sky is viewed from Earth in about  6 billion years will be like the best  fireworks display that you have ever  seen  best of all it will last for many  millions of years  as Andromeda and the Milky Way Collide  directly it will take that long for  their gas to be consumed by the  resulting Starburst  locally the sun will be evolving from a  red giant to a white dwarf  given the vast star spacing in the two  galaxies it is very unlikely that the  planetary orbits in the solar system  will be disrupted by their Collision  in this video simulation we View From  Afar the expected motions of the three  spirals in the local group from the  present day through the initial close  passive Andromeda with the Milky Way to  their final merger  note that all three galaxies appear blue  originally due to their continuing star  formation and production of massive  young stars as separate spirals  as Andromeda and the Milky Way Collide  and merge over several billion years  their Interstellar gas is completely  consumed by a starburst the young blue  stars all die off with no continuing  Replacements not possible and the merged  Galaxy ends up with the yellowish color  of an older system  note that m33  continues its star formation and  maintains its blue color as it orbits  far from the other two galaxies  the Milky Way Andromeda merger finalizes  in about 8 billion years with the  product looking like an elliptical  galaxy  m49 is an example of such an elliptical  it is located at a distance of 56  million light years and has a width of a  hundred and sixty thousand light years  the Hubble image of its innermost 50 000  light years is mostly featureless except  for a bright nucleus  this nucleus Harbors a central black  hole with a mass of 500 million Suns  overall m49 has more than 200 billion  stars and 6 000 globular clusters with  an average age of 10 billion years  its last major star formation episode  was six billion years ago it has little  Interstellar matter now  m49 is a member of the Virgo cluster of  galaxies  this Rich cluster contains more than a  thousand galaxies  m49 formed long ago from multiple  collisions of gas-rich galaxies in the  cluster  ancient Starbursts consumed all of the  interstellar matter in m49 and forged  its many globular clusters  in contrast are much sparser local group  of galaxies has allowed its gas-rich  spirals to last longer  now that the future of the Milky Way and  Andromeda is clear  names like Milko media have been  proposed for the merged elliptical that  is destined to become  of course we all want to know what will  be the fate of the solar system as a  result of this merger  there is about a 10 chance that it will  be thrown on a tidal tail into extra  Galactic space  it is less likely that it will be thrown  near or into the merged supermassive  black hole at the new galactic center  due to the vast space between the stars  there is essentially no chance that it  will collide with a star from Andromeda  thus most likely it will be thrown into  a large elliptical orbit around the new  galactic center  imagine the night sky view on Earth from  such a future solar system in about 10  billion years  the diffuse band of light across the sky  from which the Milky Way got its name is  now gone  instead there is a bright glow in one  part of the sky towards the new galactic  center  few billion years after this time there  will be some brief minor excitement when  m33 merges with the now established  milkometer Galaxy  otherwise this will be the nighttime  view on Earth for the next 100 billion  years until the tiniest Stars Burn Out  in summary the Collision of the  Andromeda galaxy with the Milky Way will  be a transformative event that changes  the very essence of our night sky  through our exploration of the Milky Way  with Hubble in this course we have seen  that it is a vast star making machine  whose spiral structure stimulates a  dynamic process that has been recycling  matter between stars and the  interstellar medium for the past 13  billion years  we have also seen how interactions  involving nearby star systems such as  the magellanic clouds can stimulate  Starbursts that more rapidly consume the  interstellar gas in a galaxy  but collision with Andromeda will  essentially consume the remaining  star-making potential of the Milky Way  over the course of three billion years  20 billion year adolescents of our  galaxy as a spiral will end and it's  much longer life as an elliptical will  begin  fortunately we live at a time when we  can still Marvel at the naked eye view  of the Milky Way stretching across the  sky from the remaining dark places on  Earth  I hope that you have enjoyed this  opportunity to explore the wonders of  our galaxy through the sharp eye of the  Hubble Space Telescope
 

